4. The Virtual Cell as a Causal World Model

A virtual cell can be represented abstractly as a forward simulator:

\mathcal{V}: (x, u, c) \mapsto \hat{x}_{t+\Delta},

where x is the current biological state, u is a perturbation or intervention, c is biological context, and \hat{x}_{t+\Delta} is the predicted future state.

However, a forward predictor is not sufficient for closed-loop control. APT requires an interventional model, not merely an observational model. We distinguish three tiers of virtual-cell capability:

1. Predictive tier. The model estimates conditional distributions from passive data:
   F_{\text{obs}}(x,u) \approx p(x_{t+1} \mid x_t, u_t).
   This tier supports reconstruction, imputation, and captioning, but not causal reasoning.
2. Counterfactual tier. The model predicts the effect of an intervention:
   F_{\text{int}}(x,u) \approx p(x_{t+1} \mid \operatorname{do}(u_t), x_t).
   This requires interventional training data and explicit causal structure.
3. World-model tier. The model generates action-conditioned trajectories over multiple steps, enabling planning and closed-loop control:
   \hat{x}_{t+1:t+H} = \mathcal{W}\big(\hat{x}_t, u_{t:t+H-1}, c\big).
   This tier is what APT requires.

The observational/interventional gap is central:

F_{\text{obs}} \neq F_{\text{int}}

in the presence of confounding or distribution shift. Current virtual-cell models can perform well on reconstruction and captioning tasks but often struggle on perturbation prediction and mechanistic question-answering. APT therefore requires that models be trained or fine-tuned on interventional data:

\mathcal{D}_{\text{int}} = \{(x_i, u_i, x_i')\},

where u_i represents an explicit perturbation, and the model is evaluated on unseen perturbations. Without this, F_{\text{int}} may not be identifiable.

Proposition 4.1 (Causal identifiability under no hidden confounding).
Assume no hidden confounding between u_t and x_{t+1} given x_t, and assume consistency and positivity. Then

p(x_{t+1}\mid \operatorname{do}(u_t),x_t)
=
p(x_{t+1}\mid u_t,x_t).

When hidden confounders exist, identification requires instrumental variables or interventional data.

Proposition 4.2 (Identification from interventional data).
Suppose the system is additive,

x_{t+1}=f_x(x_t,u_t,\theta)+U_x,

and randomized interventional data are available. Then the causal effect

\mathbb{E}[x_{t+1}\mid \operatorname{do}(u_t),x_t]

is identified as the conditional expectation of x' given (x,u) in the interventional dataset.

Causal identifiability requires more than interventional data. Biological systems are nonstationary, context-dependent, and subject to hidden confounding. APT therefore incorporates invariant causal prediction, anchor regression, and uncertainty over causal structure, rather than assuming a single fixed causal graph. The virtual cell is used as an engine inside a larger safety-constrained control architecture, not as an autonomous decision-maker.

