6. Programmable Therapeutics as Control Inputs

The intervention u_t is not restricted to RNA sequence:

u_t = (s_t, L_t, d_t, \tau_t),

where s_t is the therapeutic molecular program, L_t is the delivery architecture, d_t is the dose, and \tau_t is the timing.

For RNA therapeutics:

s_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{RNA}}, \qquad L_t \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{delivery}}.

Thus:

\text{Therapeutic program} = \text{payload} + \text{delivery} + \text{dose} + \text{time}.

This formulation makes explicit that the therapeutic object is a policy over a product space of sequence, delivery, dose, and schedule.

---

7. Delivery as a Coupled Dynamical System

Delivery is not a static parameter. It is a coupled dynamical system with its own pharmacokinetics, biodistribution, protein corona dynamics, endosomal escape, and immunogenicity.

We introduce a separate delivery state z_t:

z_{t+1} = g(z_t, L_t, \eta_t),

where L_t is the delivery architecture and \eta_t represents delivery stochasticity.

The biological response then depends on the effective cytosolic payload availability:

x_{t+1} = f\big(x_t, s_t \cdot h(z_t), \theta, w_t\big),

where h(z_t) maps delivery state to effective intracellular therapeutic activity.

This formulation explains why delivery co-design is difficult. The overall optimization is bilevel:

\min_{L \in \mathcal{L}} \mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(L),

subject to:

\mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(L) = \min_{s,d,\tau} D\big(F(x,z), x^*\big) + \lambda C(u),

where D measures deviation from the desired state and C is a cost function.

The outer loop over L is slow and manufacturing-constrained. The inner loop over s,d,\tau is faster and more programmable. Programmable lipid nanoparticles provide concrete control handles: corona engineering can detarget the liver and retarget other tissues, SORT lipids enable organ-selective delivery, and barcoded LNP libraries permit empirical identification of the mapping from delivery composition to cell-type tropism.

