9. Therapeutic Model-Predictive Control

At each decision time t, the system solves:

\min_{u_{t:t+H}} \sum_{k=0}^{H} \left[ D(x_{t+k}, x^*) + \lambda C(u_{t+k}) \right],

subject to:

x_{t+k+1} = F(x_{t+k}, u_{t+k}, \hat{\theta}_t),

and:

x_{t+k} \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}, \qquad u_{t+k} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}}.

Only the first intervention is executed:

u_t = u_t^*.

The system then observes the biological response and recomputes.

Thus:

\text{predict} \rightarrow \text{intervene} \rightarrow \text{observe} \rightarrow \text{recalculate}.

This is the core closed-loop principle of APT.

Theorem 9.1 (Recursive feasibility of MPC).
If the MPC problem is feasible at time 0, then it is feasible at every subsequent time under the receding-horizon policy, provided that a terminal controller \kappa_f(x) exists such that for all x\in\mathcal{S}_f,

f(x,\kappa_f(x))\in\mathcal{S}_f,

and

V_f(f(x,\kappa_f(x)))-V_f(x)\le -\ell(x,\kappa_f(x)).

Proof sketch.
Let the optimal control sequence at time t be u_{t:t+H-1}^*, with associated state trajectory ending in x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f. At time t+1, a feasible candidate is

u_{t+1:t+H-1}^*,\ u_{t+H}=\kappa_f(x_{t+H}).

By the terminal invariance assumption, this candidate satisfies all constraints, so feasibility is preserved.

Theorem 9.2 (Stability of MPC).
Under the assumptions above and a positive definite stage cost, the origin is asymptotically stable for the closed-loop MPC system.

Proof sketch.
Define the value function

J^*(x)=\min_{u} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\ell(x_k,u_k)+V_f(x_H).

By the Bellman inequality and terminal cost condition,

J^*(x_{t+1})-J^*(x_t)\le -\ell(x_t,u_t).

Since \ell is positive definite, J^* is a Lyapunov function, and asymptotic stability follows.

