12. Open Problems in Therapeutic Control Theory

Rather than claiming a general safety theorem, APT identifies a set of open problems that must be solved to make programmable therapeutics mathematically rigorous.

Problem 1: Patient-Specific Adaptation Convergence
Under what conditions does the digital twin update

\hat{\theta}_{t+1} = \hat{\theta}_t + \Delta\theta(y_t, \hat{x}_t)

converge to the true patient parameters \theta_i? This requires identifiability conditions for nonlinear partially observed systems with sparse measurements.

Problem 2: Biological Controllability for RNA Interventions
Define a controllability measure for RNA-based actuators acting on gene regulatory networks. The controllability Gramian for a linearized system is well-defined:

W_c = \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} A^k B B^T (A^T)^k.

But for nonlinear gene regulatory networks, global controllability is harder to characterize.

Problem 3: Generalization Bounds for Virtual Cells Under Distribution Shift
What are the limits of interventional prediction when a virtual cell is transferred to unseen patient contexts? This requires non-asymptotic generalization bounds for counterfactual inference.

Problem 4: Control Lyapunov Functions for Gene Regulatory Networks
Can we construct control Lyapunov functions for common motifs in gene regulation, such as feedback loops, feed-forward loops, and bistable switches? If so, closed-loop stability can be certified for classes of RNA interventions.

These problems are more useful than a single theorem candidate because they invite collaboration between control theorists, computational biologists, and experimentalists.

