18. Broader Implications

The ultimate significance of programmable therapeutics is not that humans will suddenly possess a universal cure.

The more plausible transformation is:

\text{Medicine}: \text{Discovery} \rightarrow \text{Design} \rightarrow \text{Control}.

The first revolution gave medicine increasingly powerful molecules. The second gives medicine increasingly programmable biological interventions. The third may give medicine adaptive therapeutic systems.

In that future, the therapeutic object is no longer necessarily a fixed drug. It becomes a therapeutic policy: a sequence of interventions selected according to the evolving state of the biological system.

---

19. Central Hypothesis

We propose the following central hypothesis:

A sufficiently accurate, uncertainty-aware model of a biological system, combined with programmable therapeutic actuators, continuous biological observation, and safety-constrained adaptive control, can transform selected areas of medicine from static intervention toward closed-loop therapeutic regulation.

This hypothesis is intentionally narrower than the claim that programmable medicine will cure almost anything. The objective is to determine which biological systems are programmable, how programmability can be quantified, and what mathematical conditions are required to control them safely.

