Appendix B. Biological System Model, Observability, and Controllability

B.1 State-space representation

The biological system is modeled as

x_{t+1}=f(x_t,u_t,\theta)+w_t,

y_t=h(x_t)+v_t,

where x_t\in\mathbb{R}^{n_x}, u_t\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_u}, \theta\in\mathbb{R}^{n_\theta}, w_t\in\mathbb{R}^{n_x}, y_t\in\mathbb{R}^{n_y}, and v_t\in\mathbb{R}^{n_y}.

B.2 Linearization and local Gramians

Let (\bar x,\bar u) be a nominal trajectory satisfying \bar x_{t+1}=f(\bar x_t,\bar u_t,\theta). The linearized system is

\Delta x_{t+1}=A_t\Delta x_t+B_t\Delta u_t+w_t,

\Delta y_t=C_t\Delta x_t+v_t,

with

A_t=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(\bar x_t,\bar u_t),\quad
B_t=\frac{\partial f}{\partial u}(\bar x_t,\bar u_t),\quad
C_t=\frac{\partial h}{\partial x}(\bar x_t).

For a time-invariant linearization (A,B,C), the finite-horizon observability Gramian is

W_o=\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}(A^\top)^k C^\top C A^k,

and the finite-horizon controllability Gramian is

W_c=\sum_{k=0}^{H-1} A^k B B^\top (A^\top)^k.

B.3 Proposition: local observability and controllability

Proposition B.1.
If \operatorname{rank}(W_o)=n_x, then the linearized system is observable on the horizon H. If \operatorname{rank}(W_c)=n_x, then the linearized system is controllable on the horizon H.

Proof.
The statement is standard. Observability of the linear time-invariant system is equivalent to

C A^k v=0\quad \forall k=0,\ldots,H-1

only for v=0. This condition is equivalent to v^\top W_o v>0 for all nonzero v, hence \operatorname{rank}(W_o)=n_x. Controllability follows by duality, using W_c. \square

B.4 Nonlinear observability

For nonlinear systems, local observability along a trajectory can be assessed via the empirical observability Gramian

W_o^{\text{emp}}=\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}
\frac{\partial y_{t+k}}{\partial x_t}^\top
\frac{\partial y_{t+k}}{\partial x_t}.

Global nonlinear observability requires stronger rank conditions on Lie derivatives. APT uses local empirical Gramians as a practical first-order assessment.

B.5 Observability bottleneck

The observability rank condition is necessary but not sufficient for clinically useful state estimation. In practice, sparse, delayed, and destructive measurements reduce the effective observability of many state directions. APT therefore treats observability as a first-class constraint and requires task-relevant observability rather than full-state observability.

