Appendix E. Safety, Predictive Filters, and Formal Guarantees

E.1 Discrete-time control barrier functions

Let b:\mathbb{R}^{n_x}\to\mathbb{R} be continuously differentiable, and define the safe set

\mathcal{S}=\{x:b(x)\ge 0\}.

A discrete-time control barrier function (CBF) condition is

b(x_{t+1})-b(x_t)\ge -\alpha b(x_t),

with \alpha\in(0,1].

E.2 Lemma: forward invariance under CBF condition

If the CBF condition holds for all t, and b(x_0)\ge 0, then b(x_t)\ge 0 for all t\ge 0.

Proof.
The condition implies

b(x_{t+1})\ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t).

Since 1-\alpha\ge 0, if b(x_t)\ge 0, then b(x_{t+1})\ge 0. The result follows by induction. \square

E.3 Predictive safety filter

At time t, given a proposed intervention u_t^*, the safety filter solves

u_t=\arg\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \|u-u_t^*\|^2

subject to

b(\hat x_{t+1\mid t})\ge (1-\alpha)b(\hat x_t),

u\in\mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}},

where \hat x_{t+1\mid t}=\hat f(\hat x_t,u).

If the optimization is infeasible, the filter applies a fallback policy u_{\text{safe}} known to keep the system in \mathcal{S}.

E.4 Split conformal prediction

Let \{(x_i,u_i,x_i')\}_{i=1}^n be exchangeable interventional data. Split the data into a training set and a calibration set. Fit a predictor \hat f on the training set. Define the nonconformity score

s_i=\|x_i'-\hat f(x_i,u_i)\|.

Let s_{(q)} be the \lceil(1-\epsilon)(n_{\text{cal}}+1)\rceil/n_{\text{cal}}-quantile of the calibration scores. Then the prediction set is

C(x,u)=\left\{z:\|z-\hat f(x,u)\|\le s_{(q)}\right\}.

E.5 Theorem: conformal coverage guarantee

Under exchangeability of the calibration and test points,

P\left(x_{t+1}\in C(x_t,u_t)\right)\ge 1-\epsilon.

Proof.
This is the standard split conformal prediction theorem. The rank of the test nonconformity score among the calibration scores is uniformly distributed under exchangeability, giving the bound. \square

E.6 Theorem: APT safety filter with conformal prediction

Assume:

1. The biological dynamics are
   x_{t+1}=f(x_t,u_t)+w_t,
   \]  
   with f Lipschitz in x with constant L_f.
2. The conformal set C(x_t,u_t) has coverage at least 1-\epsilon_t at each step, where \epsilon_t=\epsilon/H.
3. The barrier b is Lipschitz with constant L_b.
4. The safety filter enforces
   b(\tilde{x}_{t+1})\ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t)
   \]  
   for all \tilde{x}_{t+1}\in C(x_t,u_t).

Then

P\left(x_t\in\mathcal{S}\quad \forall t\in[0,H]\right)\ge 1-\epsilon.

Proof.
At each step, with probability at least 1-\epsilon_t, the true next state satisfies

x_{t+1}\in C(x_t,u_t).

On that event, the safety filter condition implies

b(x_{t+1})\ge (1-\alpha)b(x_t).

Since \alpha\in(0,1], if b(x_t)\ge 0, then b(x_{t+1})\ge 0. By induction, the safe set is maintained. By the union bound,

P\left(\exists t: x_{t+1}\notin C(x_t,u_t)\right)
\le \sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\epsilon_t
= \epsilon.

Therefore, the overall safety probability is at least 1-\epsilon. \square

E.7 Formal verification of clinical constraints

Clinical safety constraints can be expressed as linear temporal logic formulas over observed and estimated variables. For example,

\square\,(x_{\text{ALT}}\le T_{\text{ALT}}),

or

\square\,((B<B_{\text{crit}})\to \Diamond_{\le \Delta t}(\text{suspend})).

These formulas can be checked by satisfiability modulo theories (SMT) solvers over the decision layer, while learned models are confined to state estimation. This separation allows formal guarantees on the high-level therapeutic policy independent of the learned predictor's internal complexity.

