Appendix F. Model-Predictive Control: Recursive Feasibility and Stability

F.1 Problem formulation

At each time t, the MPC problem is

\min_{u_{t:t+H-1}}
\sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \ell(x_{t+k},u_{t+k}) + V_f(x_{t+H})

subject to

x_{t+k+1}=f(x_{t+k},u_{t+k}),

x_{t+k}\in\mathcal{S},\quad u_{t+k}\in\mathcal{U},

x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f.

F.2 Assumptions for stability

1. The stage cost \ell(x,u) is continuous, \ell(0,0)=0, and there exists \alpha_1\in\mathcal{K}_\infty such that \ell(x,u)\ge \alpha_1(\|x\|).
2. There exists a terminal controller \kappa_f(x) such that for all x\in\mathcal{S}_f,
   f(x,\kappa_f(x))\in\mathcal{S}_f,
   \]  
   and
   V_f(f(x,\kappa_f(x)))-V_f(x)\le -\ell(x,\kappa_f(x)).
3. The terminal set \mathcal{S}_f is a subset of \mathcal{S}.

F.3 Theorem: recursive feasibility

If the MPC problem is feasible at time 0, then it is feasible at every subsequent time under the receding-horizon policy.

Proof.
Let the optimal control sequence at time t be

u_{t:t+H-1}^*.

The associated state trajectory ends in x_{t+H}\in\mathcal{S}_f. At time t+1, a feasible candidate is

u_{t+1:t+H-1}^*,\ u_{t+H}=\kappa_f(x_{t+H}).

By the terminal invariance assumption, this candidate satisfies all constraints, so feasibility is preserved. \square

F.4 Theorem: stability of MPC

Under the assumptions of F.2 and recursive feasibility, the origin is asymptotically stable for the closed-loop system.

Proof sketch.
Define the value function

J^*(x)=\min_{u} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\ell(x_k,u_k)+V_f(x_H).

By the Bellman inequality and terminal cost condition,

J^*(x_{t+1})-J^*(x_t)\le -\ell(x_t,u_t).

Since \ell is positive definite, J^* is a Lyapunov function, and asymptotic stability follows. \square

F.5 Robust MPC with bounded disturbances

When process noise is bounded, w_t\in\mathcal{W}, constraint tightening is used:

x_{t+k}\in\mathcal{S}\ominus \mathcal{R}_k,

where \mathcal{R}_k is the reachable set of disturbances. Recursive feasibility and robust constraint satisfaction can be guaranteed using tube-based MPC.

