Appendix G. Delivery Co-Design as Bilevel Optimization

G.1 Formulation

The delivery co-design problem is

\min_{L\in\mathcal{L}} \mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(L)

subject to

\mathcal{J}_{\text{outer}}(L)=
\min_{s,d,\tau} D(F(x,z),x^*)+\lambda C(u),

z_{t+1}=g(z_t,L_t,\eta_t),

x_{t+1}=f(x_t,s_t\cdot h(z_t),\theta,w_t),

and safety constraints.

G.2 Existence of optimal solution

If \mathcal{L} is compact, g and f are continuous, and the inner problem has a continuous value function, then the outer problem attains a minimum.

Proof.
Compactness and continuity imply that the composition is continuous on a compact set, so the minimum is attained by the Weierstrass theorem. \square

G.3 Inner problem: necessary optimality conditions

For fixed L, the inner problem is

\min_{s,d,\tau} J(s,d,\tau)

subject to equality constraints from dynamics and inequality safety constraints. Let \lambda denote Lagrange multipliers. The Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions are

\nabla_{s,d,\tau} J + \sum_i \lambda_i \nabla g_i + \sum_j \mu_j \nabla h_j = 0,

\mu_j\ge 0,\quad \mu_j h_j=0,

where g_i are equality constraints and h_j\le 0 are inequality constraints.

G.4 Practical relaxation

Full bilevel optimization is computationally intractable for realistic RNA/LNP systems. APT therefore adopts a hierarchical relaxation:

· The outer loop selects a small set of manufacturable delivery architectures.
· The inner loop performs robust therapeutic control given delivery uncertainty.
· The policy is updated only when new delivery platforms become available.

This preserves the conceptual structure while enabling tractable computation.

